7],{"#text":3751},{"#text":1417},{"italic":3870},[3871],{"#text":2478},{"sub":3873},[3874],{"#text":3797},{"#text":3876}," were estimated from the so-called one-point method (",{"xref":3878,":@":3881},[3879],{"#text":3880},"De Kauwe et al., 2016",{"@_ref-type":984,"@_rid":3882},"B9",{"#text":3884},") using data recorded before and at the end of induction, respectively. Assimilation rate decreased during induction in some measurements made under ",{"italic":3886},[3887],{"#text":883},{"sub":3889},[3890],{"#text":905},{"#text":2632},{"#text":805},{"p":3894},[3895,3897,3900,3903,3905,3908,3910,3917,3919,3922,3925,3926,3929,3931,3934,3937,3938,3941,3943,3946],{"#text":3896},"ETR II obtained under photorespiratory condition was likely to deviate from true linear electron transport rate, leading to incorrect ",{"italic":3898},[3899],{"#text":927},{"sub":3901},[3902],{"#text":2926},{"#text":3904},". Considerations on how to model the midway decrease in ",{"italic":3906},[3907],{"#text":927},{"#text":3909}," during induction and necessary calibration of ETR II are described in detail in ",{"xref":3911,":@":3916},[3912],{"bold":3913},[3914],{"#text":3915},"Supplementary File S2",{"@_ref-type":1406,"@_rid":1407},{"#text":3918},". We compared ",{"italic":3920},[3921],{"#text":927},{"sub":3923},[3924],{"#text":2943},{"#text":1818},{"italic":3927},[3928],{"#text":1844},{"#text":3930},") against ",{"italic":3932},[3933],{"#text":927},{"sub":3935},[3936],{"#text":2926},{"#text":1818},{"italic":3939},[3940],{"#text":1844},{"#text":3942},") to determine whether photosynthetic rate was limited by Rubisco carboxylation or RuBP regeneration at time ",{"italic":3944},[3945],{"#text":1844},{"#text":2632},{"#text":963},{"@_id":3949},"s2_4",{"#text":963},{"sec":3952,":@":4002},[3953,3954,3958,3959,4001],{"#text":805},{"title":3955},[3956],{"#text":3957},"Statistical Analysis",{"#text":805},{"p":3960},[3961,3963,3966,3969,3970,3973,3976,3978,3982,3984,3987,3989,3992,3994,4000],{"#text":3962},"To determine the effects of measurement temperature condition, data were compared by one-way ANOVA test. Data were log-transformed to meet the assumptions of normality and homogeneity of variances when necessary. Otherwise, a non-parametric Kruskal-Wallis test was used. All tests were conducted using SPSS Statistics Version 20.0 (IBM Corp., New York, USA). To examine whether the variances in the induction responses between ",{"italic":3964},[3965],{"#text":883},{"sub":3967},[3968],{"#text":887},{"#text":1417},{"italic":3971},[3972],{"#text":883},{"sub":3974},[3975],{"#text":905},{"#text":3977}," were related to species-specific shade tolerance (",{"italic":3979},[3980],{"#text":3981},"S",{"#text":3983},") and DLI, we performed a two-way ANOVA analysis using ",{"italic":3985},[3986],{"#text":3981},{"#text":3988}," and DLI as the main factors and ",{"italic":3990},[3991],{"#text":3981},{"#text":3993}," × DLI as the interaction factor. The differences in induction responses were represented as the percentage change of a parameter. These tests were carried out in R version 3.5.0 (",{"xref":3995,":@":3998},[3996],{"#text":3997},"R Core Team, 2018",{"@_ref-type":984,"@_rid":3999},"B26",{"#text":1024},{"#text":963},{"@_id":4003},"s2_5",{"#text":966},{"@_id":4006,"@_sec-type":4007},"s2","materials|methods",{"#text":966},{"sec":4010,":@":8361,"anchorId":8362},[4011,4012,4016,4017,6064,6065,7212,7213,7636,7637,7811,7812,8360],{"#text":963},{"title":4013},[4014],{"#text":4015},"Results",{"#text":963},{"sec":4018,":@":6062},[4019,4020,4024,4025,4147,4148,4316,4317,5827,5828,5966,5967,6061],{"#text":805},{"title":4021},[4022],{"#text":4023},"Photosynthetic Induction Response",{"#text":805},{"p":4026},[4027,4029,4038,4040,4043,4046,4048,4051,4054,4056,4059,4062,4064,4067,4070,4071,4080,4082,4130,4132,4135,4138,4140,4143,4146],{"#text":4028},"Time courses of photosynthetic induction under three different temperature conditions are shown in ",{"xref":4030,":@":4035},[4031],{"bold":4032},[4033],{"#text":4034},"Figure 1",{"@_ref-type":4036,"@_rid":4037},"fig","f1",{"#text":4039},". After full acclimation under ",{"italic":4041},[4042],{"#text":883},{"sub":4044},[4045],{"#text":896},{"#text":4047},", both initial photosynthetic rate (",{"italic":4049},[4050],{"#text":927},{"sub":4052},[4053],{"#text":2316},{"#text":4055},") and final steady-state photosynthetic rate (",{"italic":4057},[4058],{"#text":927},{"sub":4060},[4061],{"#text":2743},{"#text":4063},") were significantly smaller than those under ",{"italic":4065},[4066],{"#text":883},{"sub":4068},[4069],{"#text":887},{"#text":2936},{"xref":4072,":@":4077},[4073],{"bold":4074},[4075],{"#text":4076},"Table 1",{"@_ref-type":4078,"@_rid":4079},"table","T1",{"#text":4081},"). The maximum rate of increase in ",{"inline-formula":4083},[4084],{"mml:math":4085,":@":4128},[4086],{"mml:mrow":4087},[4088,4091],{"mml:mi":4089},[4090],{"#text":927},{"mml:mrow":4092},[4093,4096,4125],{"mml:mo":4094},[4095],{"#text":1818},{"mml:mrow":4097},[4098],{"mml:msub":4099},[4100,4120],{"mml:mrow":4101},[4102],{"mml:mfrac":4103},[4104,4112],{"mml:mrow":4105},[4106,4109],{"mml:mi":4107},[4108],{"#text":1832},{"mml:mi":4110},[4111],{"#text":927},{"mml:mrow":4113},[4114,4117],{"mml:mi":4115},[4116],{"#text":1832},{"mml:mi":4118},[4119],{"#text":1844},{"mml:mrow":4121},[4122],{"mml:mi":4123},[4124],{"#text":1850},{"mml:mo":4126},[4127],{"#text":1854},{"@_display":1856,"@_id":4129},"im9",{"#text":4131}," under ",{"italic":4133},[4134],{"#text":883},{"sub":4136},[4137],{"#text":896},{"#text":4139}," decreased by 31–64% compared to that under ",{"italic":4141},[4142],{"#text":883},{"sub":4144},[4145],{"#text":887},{"#text":2632},{"#text":805},{"fig":4149,":@":4314},[4150,4151,4154,4155,4306,4307,4313],{"#text":799},{"label":4152},[4153],{"#text":4034},{"#text":799},{"caption":4156},[4157,4158,4305],{"#text":1886},{"p":4159},[4160,4162,4165,4169,4170,4173,4176,4180,4181,4184,4187,4191,4192,4195,4198,4202,4204,4208,4210,4